    "text_type": "opinion/personal essay",
    "text": "Two weeks before our wedding, I called my fiance and told him I wasn't sure anymore. In hindsight, I could have handled it better — a phone call at eleven at night probably wasn't the ideal moment. But I had been having second thoughts for months, and I couldn't keep pretending everything was fine.\n\nMy friends had mixed reactions. Some said I should have spoken up much sooner, before we'd already booked the venue and sent the invitations. Others thought I was simply panicking, and that plenty of people must have felt exactly the same way before their own weddings. Maybe they're right. Getting married is a huge decision, and it would be strange NOT to feel nervous.\n\nWhat surprised me most was my fiance's reaction. He didn't get angry. He said that if I'd stayed quiet just to avoid an awkward conversation, we might have ended up divorced within a few years anyway — and that would have hurt far more than one difficult phone call. He might have been more upset than he showed, but he never let it seem that way.\n\nWe postponed the wedding by six months, not canceled it. During that time, we talked more honestly than we ever had before — about money, about where we wanted to live, about whether we both actually wanted children. Looking back, I think we shouldn't have rushed into wedding planning in the first place without having those conversations. We must have assumed that love alone would sort out the rest.\n\nWe got married eventually, in a small ceremony with close family only. It wasn't the wedding either of us had originally planned, but I don't regret the delay. If I hadn't made that phone call, we could have gone through with a wedding neither of us was fully ready for. Sometimes the right decision doesn't look like progress at the time.",
